  "conclusion": "Three exact archived public-API CUDA continuations add 14,400 updates without replaying prefixes. All three fail the terminal gate at 6,400 updates. R1/R2 first reaches five consecutive passes at update 5,217 and its longest streak is 19 checks. It ends with only three terminal passes because minimum eigenvalue spread fails at update 6,350 (0.13328 < 0.15), despite a fully passing endpoint: HQ 0.947754 and covariance error 0.525637. KA2 and K3P have no full passing observations and finish with HQ 0.875977/0.896484 and covariance error 1.783269/1.599982. More unchanged updates improve all three recipes, but duration alone does not establish reliable terminal quality. The 400-update schedule stays fixed, so this study does not isolate schedule decay as the cause. Exact restores, cap-only extensions, final optimizer counts, complete consumed RNG checkpoints, matching continuation target batches and saved-sample checks pass. Original 1,600-update FAIL grades and the current leaderboard remain unchanged.",
  "next_action": "Stop this bounded duration round. A separately declared schedule-horizon comparison at a fixed 6,400-update cap is the next useful option for KA2/K3P. R1/R2 also needs local spread retention assessed. Preserve recipes, prior, target, architecture, seed 0, cadence and full gates; reuse the saved longer-budget baseline. No automatic follow-up, seed study, qualification or default adoption.",
